<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter13_is-a-and-has-a-relationships">
    <title>Is-A and Has-A Relationships</title>

    <p>
        Object-oriented design often begins by asking how two types are related.
        Does one type represent a specialized kind of another type, or does one
        object simply contain and use another object? These questions lead to two
        important relationships: <q>is-a</q> and <q>has-a</q>.
    </p>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_is_a_relationship">
        <title>The Is-A Relationship</title>

        <p>
            An <term>is-a relationship</term> means that one type is a specialized
            version of another type. For example, a circle is a two-dimensional
            shape, and an hourly employee is an employee. In C++, an is-a
            relationship can be represented with public inheritance.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter13_is_a_relationship">
            <title>A derived class adds specialized behavior</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter13_is_a_relationship"
                     language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Employee {
public:
    void report_role() const {
        std::cout << "Employee\n";
    }
};

class HourlyEmployee : public Employee {
public:
    void report_pay_type() const {
        std::cout << "Paid by the hour\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    HourlyEmployee employee;

    employee.report_role();
    employee.report_pay_type();

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            <c>HourlyEmployee</c> inherits the public member function
            <c>report_role()</c> from <c>Employee</c>. It also has its own member
            function, <c>report_pay_type()</c>. The derived type is more specific,
            but it remains usable as an employee.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_has_a_relationship">
        <title>The Has-A Relationship</title>

        <p>
            A <term>has-a relationship</term> means that one object contains or
            uses another object as one of its parts. This design technique is
            called <term>composition</term>. A car has an engine, a report has a
            formatter, and a student account has a balance.
        </p>

        <listing xml:id="listing_chapter13_has_a_relationship">
            <title>A car contains and uses an engine</title>
            <program xml:id="program_chapter13_has_a_relationship"
                     language="cpp" interactive="yes" line-numbers="yes">
            <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

class Engine {
public:
    void start() const {
        std::cout << "Engine started\n";
    }
};

class Car {
private:
    Engine engine;

public:
    void start_car() const {
        engine.start();
        std::cout << "Car started\n";
    }
};

int main() {
    Car car;
    car.start_car();

    return 0;
}
            ]]></code>
            </program>
        </listing>

        <p>
            A <c>Car</c> is not a kind of <c>Engine</c>. Instead, a
            <c>Car</c> has an <c>Engine</c>. The <c>Car</c> delegates the engine's
            work to its <c>engine</c> member.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_comparing_relationships">
        <title>Comparing the Two Relationships</title>

        <table xml:id="table_chapter13_is_a_has_a_comparison">
            <title>Is-a and has-a relationships</title>
            <tabular top="major" left="major" bottom="major" right="major">
                <col width="22%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <col width="28%"/>
                <col width="25%"/>
                <row header="yes">
                    <cell><p>Relationship</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Meaning</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Example</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Common C++ design</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Is-a</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>One type is a specialized version of another.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>A circle is a shape.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Public inheritance</p></cell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <cell><p>Has-a</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>One object contains or uses another object.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>A car has an engine.</p></cell>
                    <cell><p>Composition</p></cell>
                </row>
            </tabular>
        </table>

        <p>
            The wording of the relationship is a useful design test. If the
            sentence <q>A <emphasis>derived type</emphasis> is a
            <emphasis>base type</emphasis></q> is not accurate, inheritance is
            probably the wrong tool. If the sentence <q>An object has a component</q>
            is accurate, composition may be the better choice.
        </p>
    </subsection>

    <subsection xml:id="chapter13_subsection_design_guidance">
        <title>Design Guidance</title>

        <p>
            Composition is often easier to change because the containing class
            depends on a component's public interface rather than becoming part of
            the component's type hierarchy. Inheritance is appropriate when the
            derived object must be usable wherever the base type is expected. The
            next sections examine both choices more closely.
        </p>

        <p>
            The following visualization shows composition: a <c>Car</c> object
            contains an <c>Engine</c> object and asks that engine to start.
        </p>

        <interactive xml:id="interactive_chapter13_is_a_has_a_relationship"
                     iframe="https://pythontutor.com/iframe-embed.html#code=%23include%20%3Ciostream%3E%0A%0Aclass%20Engine%20%7B%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20void%20start%28%29%20const%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22Engine%20started%5Cn%22%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aclass%20Car%20%7B%0Aprivate%3A%0A%20%20%20%20Engine%20engine%3B%0A%0Apublic%3A%0A%20%20%20%20void%20start_car%28%29%20const%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20engine.start%28%29%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20std%3A%3Acout%20%3C%3C%20%22Car%20started%5Cn%22%3B%0A%20%20%20%20%7D%0A%7D%3B%0A%0Aint%20main%28%29%20%7B%0A%20%20%20%20Car%20car%3B%0A%20%20%20%20car.start_car%28%29%3B%0A%20%20%20%20return%200%3B%0A%7D&amp;codeDivHeight=360&amp;codeDivWidth=420&amp;cppShowMemAddrs=true&amp;curInstr=0&amp;origin=opt-frontend.js&amp;py=cpp_g%2B%2B9.3.0"
                     width="100%"
                     aspect="16:10">
        </interactive>
    </subsection>

    <exercises xml:id="exercises_chapter13_is_a_has_a_relationship">
        <title>Exercises</title>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_is_a_has_a_1">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which relationship best describes a <c>Library</c> containing
                    several <c>Book</c> objects?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes">
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p>Has-a</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>A library contains or uses books, so composition is appropriate.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Is-a</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>A library is not a specialized kind of book.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Overrides-a</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Overriding describes replacing inherited behavior.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>Copies-a</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Copying is not the relationship represented here.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_is_a_has_a_multiple">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Which statements correctly describe composition in the car
                    example? Select all that apply.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <choices randomize="yes" multiple-correct="yes">
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p><c>Car</c> contains an <c>Engine</c> member.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>This is a has-a relationship.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p><c>Car</c> must inherit from <c>Engine</c>.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Composition does not require inheritance.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice correct="yes">
                    <statement><p><c>Car</c> can delegate engine work to its member.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>Delegation is a common use of composition.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
                <choice>
                    <statement><p>The contained <c>Engine</c> is automatically a base-class subobject.</p></statement>
                    <feedback><p>A member object is a component, not a base-class subobject.</p></feedback>
                </choice>
            </choices>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_is_a_has_a_2">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A <c>Circle</c> is a specialized kind of <c>Shape2D</c>.
                    Which design relationship expresses this idea?
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer><p>An is-a relationship, commonly represented with public inheritance.</p></answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_is_a_has_a_3">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    In the car example, identify the component object and the
                    operation delegated to it.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    The <c>Car</c> contains an <c>Engine</c> object named
                    <c>engine</c>, and <c>start_car()</c> delegates work by calling
                    <c>engine.start()</c>.
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise xml:id="exercise_chapter13_is_a_has_a_parsons"
                  number="yes"
                  adaptive="yes"
                  indentation="show">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Arrange the blocks to define a <c>Car</c> that contains an
                    <c>Engine</c> and delegates starting to that engine.
                </p>
            </statement>
            <blocks randomize="yes">
                <block order="1">
                    <cline>class Car {</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="2">
                    <cline>private:</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="3">
                    <cline>    Engine engine;</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="4">
                    <cline>public:</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="5">
                    <cline>    void start_car() const {</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="6">
                    <cline>        engine.start();</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="7">
                    <cline>    }</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="8">
                    <cline>};</cline>
                </block>
                <block order="1" correct="no">
                    <cline>class Car : public Engine {</cline>
                </block>
            </blocks>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>
</section>
